-- title:
--   Dropping a minimal generator of an ideal over a local ring
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative local ring with maximal ideal $\mathfrak m =$ `IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R`, let $n$ be a natural number, and let $g : \mathrm{Fin}(n+1) \to R$ be a family of $n+1$ elements of $R$, generating the ideal $I = \mathrm{span}(\mathrm{range}\,g)$. Let $x \in R$ be an element of $I$ which does not lie in the product $\mathfrak m \cdot I$ (the submodule-scalar product of the maximal ideal with $I$). Then there exists a family $g' : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to R/(x)$ of $n$ elements of the quotient of $R$ by the principal ideal generated by $x$ such that the image of $I$ under the quotient map $R \to R/(x)$, in the sense of `Ideal.map`, equals the ideal of $R/(x)$ generated by the range of $g'$. Thus the image of an ideal with $n+1$ generators in the quotient by one of its elements lying outside $\mathfrak m I$ is generated by $n$ elements; no minimality claim about the original family $g$, and no converse, is asserted.
--
--   This is the standard consequence of Nakayama's lemma that an element of $I$ outside $\mathfrak m I$ may be taken as a member of a generating family, so that killing it lowers the number of generators of $I$ by one. It is used in [`Algebra.exists_ker_eq_span_of_surjective_truncated_of_ker_eq_span`](thm.html#Algebra.exists_ker_eq_span_of_surjective_truncated_of_ker_eq_span), in the reduction of a presentation of a local algebra to one with fewer variables or relations.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Ideal_exists_map_mk_span_singleton_eq_span_of_not_mem_smul.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
